There are three simple steps to solve this problem using the formula for permutations since the order of selection matters. The formula needed is P(n, k) = \frac{n!}{(n-k)!}, where n is the total number of items to choose from, and k is the number of items to choose. In this scenario, there are 20 members available, and 3 positions to fill: president, vice-president, and treasurer. No member can hold more than one office.

Step 1: Apply the formula for permutations for P(20, 4).

\begin{align*}
P(20, 4) &= \frac{(20)!}{(20-4)!}\\
&= \frac{20 \times 19 \times 18 \times 17}{1 \times 2 \times 3 \times 4} \\
&= 20 \times 19 \times 18 \times 17
\end{align*}

Step 2: Calculate the product of 20, 19, 18, and 17.

\begin{align*}
20 \times 19 &= 380 \\
380 \times 18 &= 6840 \\
6840 \times 17 &= 116,280
\end{align*}

Therefore, there are 116,280 ways to select a president, a vice-president, and a treasurer from 20 members if no member can hold more than one office.

\boxed{116280}

